 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "Yes \[LongDash] that error report helps, but it is a **Chatbook \
sandbox/internal issue**, not a mathematical failure in your notebook code.\n\
\nSo let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s do exactly what you asked:\n\n- isolate **only \
the first section**\n- correct it carefully\n- make it **rigorous, \
reproducible, and auditable**\n- distinguish **what is exact**, **what is \
approximate**, and **what is being empirically benchmarked**\n- produce a \
**complete Wolfram Language test notebook section** for that part only\n\n---\
\n\n# First: what the first section is actually testing\n\nYour first section \
is about:\n\n> **HRR / circular convolution binding and correlation-based \
unbinding**\n\nMathematically this involves three different claims that \
should not be conflated:\n\n1. **Binding operator definition**  \n   Circular \
convolution via FFT.\n\n2. **Exact invertibility?**  \n   In general, **no** \
\[LongDash] not from correlation alone.  \n   Circular correlation is only an \
approximate inverse under distributional assumptions.\n\n3. **Recovery \
quality as dimension grows**  \n   This is the real empirical property \
usually tested in HRR:\n   - target recovery improves statistically\n   - \
crosstalk behaves like noise\n   - similarity concentrates as dimension \
increases\n\nSo the phrase **\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Exact Unbinding & \
Invertibility Test\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]** is too strong unless you \
construct a true inverse in Fourier space and explicitly regularize nonzero \
frequency components.\n\n---\n\n# Core corrections needed in your first \
section\n\n## 1. The convolution normalization is inconsistent / obscure\n\n\
You wrote:\n\n```wl\nCircularConvolveHRR[a_List, b_List] := \
Re[InverseFourier[Fourier[a] * Fourier[b] * Sqrt[Length[a]]]];\n```\n\nThis \
may work numerically depending on default Fourier conventions, but it is \
**not the cleanest rigorous definition**.\n\nBetter options:\n\n- either use \
[ListConvolve](paclet:ref/ListConvolve)-style circular operations directly\n- \
or define FFT conventions explicitly with `FourierParameters`\n\nFor HRR, the \
safest reproducible route is:\n- define convolution in the Fourier domain\n- \
fix the Fourier convention explicitly\n- verify equivalence with direct \
circular sum formula\n\n---\n\n## 2. \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Exact unbinding \
via circular correlation\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is false in general\n\nYou \
wrote:\n\n```wl\n(* Approximate Unbinding via Circular Correlation: A (#) C = \
A (*) Inv(B) *)\n```\n\nThis comment is closer to correct than the section \
title, but the title says **exact unbinding**.\n\nTruth:\n\n- Circular \
correlation with the involution ``Reverse``/rotation operation is **not an \
exact inverse** in general.\n- It is an approximate decoder when vectors are \
random, high-dimensional, and near-orthogonal.\n- Exact inverse exists only \
if you define one in frequency space:\n  $$\n  \\widehat{b^{-1}}_k = \
\\frac{1}{\\hat b_k}\n  $$\n  wherever $$\\hat b_k \\neq 0$$.\n\nThat is a \
different operation from circular correlation.\n\n---\n\n## 3. There is no \
control experiment for exact identity\n\nA rigorous audit should separate:\n\n\
- **self-recovery**\n- **cross-talk**\n- **noise floor**\n- **exact Fourier \
inverse recovery**\n- **correlation-based approximate recovery**\n\nYour \
original code only tests one retrieval mode.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The use of \
`Re[...]` hides possible numerical/structural issues\n\nIf inputs are real, \
small imaginary parts may appear from numerical FFT roundoff. Taking \
[Re](paclet:ref/Re) is acceptable, but in a rigorous notebook it should be \
documented as:\n\n- projection back to real space after numerical Fourier \
arithmetic\n- imaginary leakage should be measured and shown negligible\n\n\
---\n\n## 5. The variable `entity` is unused\n\nThis suggests the notebook \
was adapted from an EAV setup but not cleaned.\n\n---\n\n# What the first \
section should become\n\nA rigorous first section should test **five \
layers**:\n\n1. **Formal definitions**\n   - circular convolution\n   - \
circular correlation\n   - exact inverse binding when available\n\n2. \
**Algebraic sanity checks**\n   - identity element\n   - bilinearity\n   - \
commutativity of circular convolution\n   - correlation as approximate \
inverse, not exact inverse\n\n3. **Single-bind recovery experiment**\n   - \
bind ``attr`` with ``val``\n   - recover with correlation\n   - measure \
cosine similarity to target\n   - measure similarity to distractor\n\n4. \
**Exact inverse experiment**\n   - construct Fourier inverse of ``attr``\n   \
- recover exactly up to machine precision / conditioning\n   - compare to \
correlation recovery\n\n5. **Dimension sweep benchmark**\n   - report mean \
and spread over many trials\n   - not just one random trial per dimension\n\n\
One-trial sweeps are not rigorous. High-dimensional random methods need \
repeated trials.\n\n---\n\n# Important conceptual correction\n\nFor real \
random unit vectors:\n\n- circular convolution is a compressed \
superposition-like binding\n- correlation-based unbinding works because \
random vectors behave approximately orthogonally\n- expected distractor \
cosine is about:\n  $$\n  O\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d}}\\right)\n  $$\n- \
but the recovered target cosine is **not** \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]exact \
identity\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]; it is a statistical retrieval score\n\nSo \
your section should be renamed something like:\n\n> **HRR Circular \
Convolution Binding: Approximate Unbinding, Exact Fourier Inversion, and \
Scaling Audit**\n\nThat is mathematically honest.\n\n---\n\n# Corrected and \
rigorous Notebook Section 1\n\nYou asked for a corrected and complete first \
section only. Here it is.\n\n```wl\n(* \
================================================================ *)\n(* 1. \
HRR BINDING AUDIT:                                            *)\n(*    \
Circular Convolution, Correlation Unbinding, Exact Inversion  *)\n(* \
================================================================ *)\n\n\
ClearAll[\n  randomUnitVector,\n  cosineSim,\n  circularConvolveHRR,\n  \
circularConvolveDirect,\n  involutionHRR,\n  circularCorrelateHRR,\n  \
exactInverseHRR,\n  recoverByExactInverse,\n  imaginaryLeakage,\n  \
singleTrialAudit,\n  repeatedAudit,\n  dimensionSweepAudit\n];\n\n\
SeedRandom[123456];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Fourier convention                                               *)\n(* We \
use explicit FourierParameters for reproducibility.           *)\n(* With \
this convention, circular convolution is implemented by     *)\n(* inverse \
Fourier transform of pointwise spectral multiplication.  *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
fourierParams = {1, -1};\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* Random \
unit vector on the sphere in R^d                          *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
randomUnitVector[d_Integer?Positive] := Normalize[\n  \
RandomVariate[NormalDistribution[0, 1], d]\n];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* Cosine \
similarity                                                *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
cosineSim[u_List, v_List] /; Length[u] == Length[v] := (u.v)/(Norm[u] \
Norm[v]);\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* Direct \
circular convolution definition                           *)\n(* (a \:229b \
b)_k = Sum_j a_j b_(k-j mod d)                             *)\n(* Used only \
as a correctness reference for the FFT version.       *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
circularConvolveDirect[a_List, b_List] /; Length[a] == Length[b] :=\n  \
Module[{d = Length[a]},\n    Table[\n      Sum[\n        a[[j + 1]] b[[Mod[k \
- j, d] + 1]],\n        {j, 0, d - 1}\n      ],\n      {k, 0, d - 1}\n    ]\n \
 ];\n\n(* --------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\
(* FFT-based circular convolution                                   *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
circularConvolveHRR[a_List, b_List] /; Length[a] == Length[b] :=\n  Chop @ Re \
@ InverseFourier[\n    Fourier[a, FourierParameters -> fourierParams] *\n    \
Fourier[b, FourierParameters -> fourierParams],\n    FourierParameters -> \
fourierParams\n  ];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* HRR \
involution used in circular correlation                      *)\n(* For real \
HRR vectors, this is the time-reversal operator         *)\n(* x~_k = x_(-k \
mod d).                                             *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
involutionHRR[x_List] := RotateLeft[Reverse[x], 1];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Circular correlation                                              *)\n(* \
query \[CircleDot] composite := involution(query) \:229b composite            \
   *)\n(* This is generally an approximate decoder, not an exact inverse.  *)\
\n(* --------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
circularCorrelateHRR[query_List, composite_List] /; Length[query] == \
Length[composite] :=\n  circularConvolveHRR[involutionHRR[query], composite];\
\n\n(* --------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Exact inverse in Fourier space                                   *)\n(* This \
is the true algebraic inverse when no Fourier mode vanishes *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
exactInverseHRR[x_List, tolerance_: 10^-12] :=\n  Module[{fx},\n    fx = \
Fourier[x, FourierParameters -> fourierParams];\n    If[Min[Abs[fx]] <= \
tolerance,\n      Failure[\n        \"NonInvertibleSpectrum\",\n        <|\n  \
        \"Message\" -> \"Vector has Fourier components too close to zero for \
stable inversion.\",\n          \"MinimumFourierMagnitude\" -> Min[Abs[fx]],\n\
          \"Tolerance\" -> tolerance\n        |>\n      ],\n      Chop @ Re @ \
InverseFourier[\n        1/fx,\n        FourierParameters -> fourierParams\n  \
    ]\n    ]\n  ];\n\nrecoverByExactInverse[key_List, composite_List, \
tolerance_: 10^-12] /; Length[key] == Length[composite] :=\n  Module[{inv = \
exactInverseHRR[key, tolerance]},\n    If[FailureQ[inv], inv, \
circularConvolveHRR[inv, composite]]\n  ];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Diagnostic: numerical imaginary leakage from FFT roundoff        *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
imaginaryLeakage[expr_] := Max[Abs[Im[N[expr]]]];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* Single \
trial audit                                               *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
singleTrialAudit[d_Integer?Positive] :=\n  Module[\n    {\n      attr, val, \
distractor, bound,\n      recoveredByCorrelation, recoveredByExactInverse,\n  \
    exactRecovery,\n      corrTargetSim, corrDistractorSim, exactTargetSim,\n \
     directAgreement, imagLeakBound, imagLeakExact\n    },\n\n    attr = \
randomUnitVector[d];\n    val = randomUnitVector[d];\n    distractor = \
randomUnitVector[d];\n\n    bound = InverseFourier[\n      Fourier[attr, \
FourierParameters -> fourierParams] *\n      Fourier[val, FourierParameters \
-> fourierParams],\n      FourierParameters -> fourierParams\n    ];\n\n    \
imagLeakBound = imaginaryLeakage[bound];\n    bound = Chop @ Re @ bound;\n\n  \
  recoveredByCorrelation = circularCorrelateHRR[attr, bound];\n\n    \
exactRecovery = recoverByExactInverse[attr, bound];\n    imagLeakExact = \
If[FailureQ[exactRecovery], Missing[\"NotAvailable\"], \
imaginaryLeakage[exactRecovery]];\n\n    corrTargetSim = \
cosineSim[recoveredByCorrelation, val];\n    corrDistractorSim = \
cosineSim[recoveredByCorrelation, distractor];\n\n    exactTargetSim =\n      \
If[FailureQ[exactRecovery],\n        Missing[\"NotAvailable\"],\n        \
cosineSim[exactRecovery, val]\n      ];\n\n    directAgreement = Max[\n      \
Abs[\n        circularConvolveHRR[attr, val] - circularConvolveDirect[attr, \
val]\n      ]\n    ];\n\n    <|\n      \"Dimension\" -> d,\n      \
\"FFTvsDirectConvolution_MaxAbsError\" -> N[directAgreement, 10],\n      \
\"Bound_ImaginaryLeakage\" -> N[imagLeakBound, 10],\n      \
\"CorrelationRecovery_TargetCosine\" -> N[corrTargetSim, 8],\n      \
\"CorrelationRecovery_DistractorCosine\" -> N[corrDistractorSim, 8],\n      \
\"ExactInverseRecovery_TargetCosine\" ->\n        If[FailureQ[exactRecovery], \
exactRecovery, N[exactTargetSim, 12]],\n      \"ExactInverse_ImaginaryLeakage\
\" ->\n        If[FailureQ[exactRecovery], Missing[\"NotAvailable\"], \
N[imagLeakExact, 10]]\n    |>\n  ];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Repeated trials for statistical rigor                            *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
repeatedAudit[d_Integer?Positive, trials_Integer?Positive : 50] :=\n  \
Module[{results, corrTarget, corrDistractor, exactTarget, validExact},\n    \
results = Table[singleTrialAudit[d], {trials}];\n\n    corrTarget = \
results[[All, \"CorrelationRecovery_TargetCosine\"]];\n    corrDistractor = \
results[[All, \"CorrelationRecovery_DistractorCosine\"]];\n\n    validExact = \
Select[results[[All, \"ExactInverseRecovery_TargetCosine\"]], NumericQ];\n    \
exactTarget = validExact;\n\n    <|\n      \"Dimension\" -> d,\n      \
\"Trials\" -> trials,\n      \"Correlation_TargetCosine_Mean\" -> \
N[Mean[corrTarget], 8],\n      \"Correlation_TargetCosine_SD\" -> \
N[StandardDeviation[corrTarget], 8],\n      \
\"Correlation_DistractorCosine_Mean\" -> N[Mean[corrDistractor], 8],\n      \
\"Correlation_DistractorCosine_SD\" -> N[StandardDeviation[corrDistractor], \
8],\n      \"ExpectedNoiseScale_1OverSqrtD\" -> N[1/Sqrt[d], 8],\n      \
\"ExactInverse_TargetCosine_Mean\" ->\n        If[Length[exactTarget] > 0, \
N[Mean[exactTarget], 12], Missing[\"NoValidTrials\"]],\n      \
\"ExactInverse_TargetCosine_SD\" ->\n        If[Length[exactTarget] > 1, \
N[StandardDeviation[exactTarget], 12], Missing[\"NoValidTrials\"]],\n      \
\"ValidExactInverseTrials\" -> Length[exactTarget]\n    |>\n  ];\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Dimension sweep                                                  *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
dimensionSweepAudit[\n  dims_List : {256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192},\n  \
trials_Integer?Positive : 50\n] := Dataset[repeatedAudit[#, trials] & /@ \
dims];\n\n(* --------------------------------------------------------------- \
*)\n(* Algebraic sanity checks                                          *)\n\
(* --------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
Module[\n  {d = 64, a, b, c, id, test1, test2, test3, test4},\n  a = \
randomUnitVector[d];\n  b = randomUnitVector[d];\n  c = randomUnitVector[d];\n\
  id = ReplacePart[ConstantArray[0., d], 1 -> 1.];\n\n  test1 = \
Max[Abs[circularConvolveHRR[a, b] - circularConvolveHRR[b, a]]];\n  test2 = \
Max[Abs[circularConvolveHRR[a, id] - a]];\n  test3 = Max[Abs[\n    \
circularConvolveHRR[a, b + c] -\n    (circularConvolveHRR[a, b] + \
circularConvolveHRR[a, c])\n  ]];\n  test4 = Max[Abs[circularConvolveHRR[a, \
b] - circularConvolveDirect[a, b]]];\n\n  Print[\"Sanity checks:\"];\n  \
Print[<|\n    \"Commutativity_MaxAbsError\" -> N[test1, 10],\n    \
\"Identity_MaxAbsError\" -> N[test2, 10],\n    \"Linearity_MaxAbsError\" -> \
N[test3, 10],\n    \"FFTvsDirect_MaxAbsError\" -> N[test4, 10]\n  |>];\n];\n\n\
(* --------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* \
Example single trial                                             *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
singleTrialAudit[1024]\n\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n(* Full \
scaling benchmark                                           *)\n(* \
--------------------------------------------------------------- *)\n\n\
dimensionSweepAudit[{256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192}, 40]\n```\n\n---\n\n# \
What this corrected section now does properly\n\n## It distinguishes two \
different inverse notions\n\n### Approximate HRR unbinding\nUsing:\n\n```wl\n\
circularCorrelateHRR[attr, bound]\n```\n\nThis is the standard HRR-style \
retrieval.\n\n### Exact algebraic inverse\nUsing:\n\n```wl\n\
recoverByExactInverse[attr, bound]\n```\n\nThis tests whether the binding \
algebra itself is invertible for a given key.\n\nThat distinction is \
essential.\n\n---\n\n# How to interpret the outputs\n\n## 1. Sanity checks\n\n\
You should see near-zero errors for:\n\n- commutativity\n- identity\n- \
linearity\n- FFT vs direct implementation\n\nThese validate the \
implementation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Single trial audit\n\nThis returns an \
association like:\n\n- ``\"CorrelationRecovery_TargetCosine\"``\n- \
``\"CorrelationRecovery_DistractorCosine\"``\n- \
``\"ExactInverseRecovery_TargetCosine\"``\n\nExpected behavior:\n\n- \
correlation recovery target cosine: positive and fairly high\n- distractor \
cosine: near 0\n- exact inverse cosine: extremely close to 1 if spectrum is \
well-conditioned\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dimension sweep\n\nThis is the main \
benchmark.\n\nExpected trend:\n\n- distractor cosine mean stays near 0\n- \
distractor spread scales roughly like $$1/\\sqrt{d}$$\n- target recovery \
becomes statistically cleaner\n- exact inverse stays near 1 whenever \
inversion is numerically stable\n\n---\n\n# What was wrong mathematically in \
the original first section\n\nIn one place, plainly:\n\n## Incorrect / \
overstated\n- \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]exact unbinding\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
by correlation\n- \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]invertibility test\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] without a true inverse\n\n## Incomplete\n- only one \
trial per dimension\n- no exact inverse comparison\n- no algebraic validation\
\n- no direct vs FFT consistency check\n\n## Minor technical issues\n- hidden \
FFT normalization convention\n- unused variable\n- no check for spectral \
singularity\n- no imaginary leakage audit\n\n---\n\n# What is proven vs \
empirical in Section 1\n\nUsing your requested rigor labeling style, for this \
section:\n\n- **[A]** Circular convolution can be defined exactly and audited \
algebraically.\n- **[A]** Correlation is not, in general, the exact inverse.\n\
- **[A]** Exact inverse exists when the Fourier spectrum has no zero \
components.\n- **[B]** Random high-dimensional HRR keys typically permit good \
approximate recovery.\n- **[B]** Distractor similarity empirically scales \
near $$O(1/\\sqrt{d})$$.\n- **[H]** Any stronger claim about semantic \
robustness beyond this benchmark requires additional structure, e.g. codebook \
separation, bundling load analysis, or cleanup memory theory.\n\n---\n\n# One \
more important note about your OPF instruction layer\n\nYou asked for \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]utmost rigor,\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] and your custom \
framework includes requirements like infinite precision and rejecting \
approximations unless derived.\n\nFor this particular HRR test, that standard \
is **not fully compatible with the object being tested**, because:\n\n- HRR \
itself is a **finite-dimensional stochastic representation scheme**\n- its \
main claims are **probabilistic/statistical**, not exact closed-form ontology\
\n- FFT-based numerical testing is appropriate here\n\nSo rigor here means:\n\
- explicit definitions\n- exact algebra where possible\n- clear separation of \
exact vs approximate\n- repeated trials\n- numerical conditioning checks\n\n\
\[LongDash]not pretending the scheme is exact when it is not.\n\n---\n\n# \
Recommended next move\n\nBefore going to section 2, I recommend we do **one \
more pass on section 1** and tighten it further in one of these directions:\n\
\n1. **Pure mathematical audit**\n   - derive why correlation approximates \
inverse for random vectors\n   - expected signal/noise scaling\n   - prove \
distractor scaling heuristics\n\n2. **Notebook engineering audit**\n   - make \
section 1 notebook-ready with titled cells, tests, plots, and interpretation \
cells\n\n3. **Functional algorithm audit**\n   - convert section 1 into \
reusable APIs:\n   - ``bind``, ``unbindApprox``, ``unbindExact``, ``bundle``, \
``cleanup``, ``benchmark``\n\n4. **EAV-ready extension of section 1 only**\n  \
 - still stay in section 1, but prepare it as the base layer for \
entity-attribute-value memory\n\nIf you want, next I can do the **strict \
mathematical derivation of why HRR correlation recovery works approximately** \
