         {
          TabViewBox[{{1,"\<\"Raw\"\>"->
           FrameBox[
            FrameBox[Cell[BoxData[
              PaneBox[
               
               PaneBox["\<\"/create_nb\\n# OPF Void-Polarity Extension: \
Minimal Symbolic Model\\n\\nThis notebook sets up a rigorous minimal symbolic \
framework for the five-field OPF extension with fields\\n$$\\\\{\\\\rho(x),\\\
\\nu(x),A(x),R(x),\\\\Psi(x)\\\\}$$\\nwhere:\\n\\n- $$\\\\rho$$ is positive \
information density\\n- $$\\\\nu$$ is void / compensating informational \
density\\n- $$A$$ is anisotropy-orientation structure\\n- $$R$$ is \
observational resolution\\n- $$\\\\Psi$$ is the total field state\\n\\nThe \
goal is to define:\\n\\n1. a source equation,\\n2. a void induction equation,\
\\n3. a measurement operator,\\n4. a restoration operator,\\n5. a recursive \
and continuous update law.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Design principles\\n\\nWe will \
keep the first version intentionally minimal:\\n\\n- symbolic first,\\n- \
modular,\\n- input/output compatible,\\n- extensible to variational and \
spectral analysis,\\n- suitable for later conversion into \
operator/state-space form.\\n\\nWe treat the framework as a \
composition\\n$$L_0 \\\\to L_1 \\\\to L_2$$\\nwith:\\n\\n- substrate / \
geometry\\n- informational field evolution\\n- measurement and maintained \
observables\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Core symbolic \
declarations\\n\\n```wl\\nClearAll[\\n  x, y, t, tau, n,\\n  rho, nu, \
anisotropy, resolution, psi,\\n  sourceKernel, voidKernel, measureKernel, \
restoreKernel,\\n  measurementOperator, restorationOperator, \
sourceOperator,\\n  voidInductionOperator, updateOperator, \
continuousUpdateOperator,\\n  neighborConfiguration, effectivePotential, \
totalDensity,\\n  sourceEquation, voidEquation, recursiveUpdate, \
continuousUpdate,\\n  opfState, opfInput, opfOutput\\n];\\n```\\n\\nWe use \
the following naming convention:\\n\\n- ``rho[x]`` for $$\\\\rho(x)$$\\n- \
``nu[x]`` for $$\\\\nu(x)$$\\n- ``anisotropy[x]`` for $$A(x)$$\\n- \
``resolution[x]`` for $$R(x)$$\\n- ``psi[x]`` for \
$$\\\\Psi(x)$$\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Field definitions\\n\\n```wl\\nfields = <|\
\\n  \\\"PositiveDensity\\\" -> rho,\\n  \\\"VoidDensity\\\" -> nu,\\n  \
\\\"AnisotropyField\\\" -> anisotropy,\\n  \\\"ResolutionField\\\" -> \
resolution,\\n  \\\"TotalField\\\" -> psi\\n|>;\\n```\\n\\nA minimal \
algebraic relation for total effective density \
is:\\n\\n$$D(x)=\\\\rho(x)-\\\\nu(x)$$\\n\\n```wl\\ntotalDensity[x_] := \
rho[x] - nu[x];\\n```\\n\\nA first minimal decomposition for the total field \
can be recorded symbolically \
as:\\n\\n$$\\\\Psi(x)=\\\\Psi[\\\\rho(x),\\\\nu(x),A(x),R(x)]$$\\n\\n```wl\\\
npsiRepresentation = psi[x] == psi[rho[x], nu[x], anisotropy[x], \
resolution[x]];\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Neighbor-relative anisotropy \
structure\\n\\nTo encode your opposite-side relational intuition, we \
introduce a symbolic neighbor configuration and a directional anisotropy \
field.\\n\\n```wl\\nneighborConfiguration = {\\n  {\\\"NeighborPosition\\\" \
-> y, \\\"NeighborStrength\\\" -> Subscript[s, 1]},\\n  {\\\"NeighborPosition\
\\\" -> Subscript[y, 2], \\\"NeighborStrength\\\" -> Subscript[s, \
2]}\\n};\\n```\\n\\nA generic anisotropy generator can be left \
symbolic:\\n\\n```wl\\nanisotropyGenerator[x_, neighbors_] := anisotropy[x, \
neighbors];\\n```\\n\\nLater this can become a scalar, vector, or \
tensor-valued field.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Source equation\\n\\nA minimal \
source equation should capture:\\n\\n- intrinsic positive information \
density,\\n- neighbor influence,\\n- possible diffusion or propagation,\\n- \
coupling to resolution and anisotropy.\\n\\nA general symbolic source \
operator:\\n\\n```wl\\nsourceOperator[field_, x_] := sourceOperator[field, \
anisotropy[x], resolution[x], x];\\n```\\n\\nA first symbolic source equation \
can be written \
as:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal{S}_\\\\rho[\\\\rho,A,R](x)=0$$\\n\\n```wl\\\
nsourceEquation[x_] := sourceOperator[rho, x] == 0;\\n```\\n\\nA more \
concrete prototype PDE form is:\\n\\n$$\\\\partial_\\\\tau \
\\\\rho(x,\\\\tau)=D_\\\\rho\\\\,\\\\nabla^2\\\\rho(x,\\\\tau)+F_\\\\rho(\\\\\
rho,\\\\nu,A,R;x,\\\\tau)$$\\n\\n```wl\\nprototypeSourceEquation =\\n  \
D[rho[x, tau], tau] ==\\n    Subscript[d, rho] Laplacian[rho[x, tau], {x}] \
+\\n    Subscript[f, rho][rho[x, tau], nu[x, tau], anisotropy[x, tau], \
resolution[x, tau], x, tau];\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Void induction \
equation\\n\\nThis is the key new ingredient.\\n\\nThe void field $$\\\\nu$$ \
is not treated as ordinary negative matter. It is a compensating induced \
field depending on:\\n\\n- source density,\\n- geometry of neighbors,\\n- \
measurement events,\\n- anisotropy,\\n- resolution.\\n\\nA symbolic induction \
operator:\\n\\n```wl\\nvoidInductionOperator[field_, x_] := \\n  \
voidInductionOperator[field, anisotropy[x], resolution[x], \
neighborConfiguration, x];\\n```\\n\\nMinimal symbolic void equation:\\n\\n$$\
\\\\mathcal{S}_\\\\nu[\\\\rho,\\\\nu,A,R,\\\\mathcal \
N](x)=0$$\\n\\n```wl\\nvoidEquation[x_] := voidInductionOperator[psi, x] == \
0;\\n```\\n\\nA prototype dynamic form:\\n\\n$$\\\\partial_\\\\tau \
\\\\nu(x,\\\\tau)=D_\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\nabla^2\\\\nu(x,\\\\tau)+F_\\\\nu(\\\\rho,\
\\\\nu,A,R,\\\\mathcal M,\\\\mathcal \
N;x,\\\\tau)$$\\n\\n```wl\\nprototypeVoidEquation =\\n  D[nu[x, tau], tau] ==\
\\n    Subscript[d, nu] Laplacian[nu[x, tau], {x}] +\\n    Subscript[f, \
nu][\\n      rho[x, tau], nu[x, tau], anisotropy[x, tau], resolution[x, tau],\
\\n      measurementOperator[psi, x, tau], neighborConfiguration, x, tau\\n   \
 ];\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Measurement operator\\n\\nThe measurement \
operator maps the latent field state into an observable or partially \
decohered structure.\\n\\nMinimal symbolic form:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal \
M[\\\\Psi](x)$$\\n\\n```wl\\nmeasurementOperator[field_, x_] := \
measurementOperator[field, measureKernel, x];\\n```\\n\\nIntegral-kernel \
prototype:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal M[\\\\Psi](x)=\\\\int K_M(x,y)\\\\,\\\\Psi(y)\\\
\\,dy$$\\n\\n```wl\\nmeasurementKernelForm[x_] := \\n  \
measurementOperator[psi, x] == Integrate[measureKernel[x, y] psi[y], \
y];\\n```\\n\\nIf the notebook later uses basis expansions, this can be \
replaced by projection onto eigenfunctions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Restoration \
operator\\n\\nThe restoration operator expresses your intuition that \
measurement creates a deficit / void requiring re-filling or re-balancing.\\n\
\\nMinimal symbolic form:\\n\\n$$\\\\mathcal R[\\\\mathcal \
M[\\\\Psi],\\\\nu,A,R](x)$$\\n\\n```wl\\nrestorationOperator[field_, x_] := \
\\n  restorationOperator[field, nu[x], anisotropy[x], resolution[x], \
restoreKernel, x];\\n```\\n\\nKernel \
prototype:\\n\\n```wl\\nrestorationKernelForm[x_] := \\n  \
restorationOperator[measurementOperator[psi, x], x] == \\n    \
Integrate[restoreKernel[x, y] measurementOperator[psi, y], y];\\n```\\n\\n---\
\\n\\n## 9. Recursive update law\\n\\nA discrete process-depth model \
expresses emergent time as iterative compensation:\\n\\n$$\\\\Psi_{n+1}(x)=\\\
\\Psi_n(x)-\\\\mathcal M[\\\\Psi_n](x)+\\\\mathcal R[\\\\mathcal \
M[\\\\Psi_n]](x)$$\\n\\n```wl\\nrecursiveUpdate[x_] :=\\n  psi[n + 1, x] \
==\\n    psi[n, x] - measurementOperator[psi[n, #] &, x] +\\n      \
restorationOperator[measurementOperator[psi[n, #] &, x], x];\\n```\\n\\nA \
more state-complete update can also evolve the component \
fields:\\n\\n```wl\\nopfState[n_, x_] := <|\\n  \\\"rho\\\" -> rho[n, x],\\n  \
\\\"nu\\\" -> nu[n, x],\\n  \\\"anisotropy\\\" -> anisotropy[n, x],\\n  \
\\\"resolution\\\" -> resolution[n, x],\\n  \\\"psi\\\" -> psi[n, \
x]\\n|>;\\n```\\n\\n```wl\\nupdateOperator[state_, input_, x_] := \
updateOperator[state, input, x];\\n```\\n\\n```wl\\nopfInput[n_, x_] := <|\\n \
 \\\"Neighbors\\\" -> neighborConfiguration,\\n  \\\"MeasurementEvent\\\" -> \
Subscript[m, n][x],\\n  \\\"ExternalConstraint\\\" -> Subscript[u, \
n][x]\\n|>;\\n```\\n\\n```wl\\nopfOutput[n_, x_] := <|\\n  \\\"TotalDensity\\\
\" -> totalDensity[x],\\n  \\\"MeasuredField\\\" -> \
measurementOperator[psi[n, #] &, x],\\n  \\\"RestoredField\\\" -> \
restorationOperator[measurementOperator[psi[n, #] &, x], \
x]\\n|>;\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Continuous update law\\n\\nA continuous \
process-depth version is:\\n\\n$$\\\\partial_\\\\tau \\\\Psi(x,\\\\tau)= \
-\\\\mathcal M[\\\\Psi](x,\\\\tau)+\\\\mathcal R[\\\\mathcal M[\\\\Psi]](x,\\\
\\tau)$$\\n\\n```wl\\ncontinuousUpdate[x_] :=\\n  D[psi[x, tau], tau] ==\\n   \
 -measurementOperator[psi[#, tau] &, x] +\\n      \
restorationOperator[measurementOperator[psi[#, tau] &, x], x];\\n```\\n\\nA \
richer coupled continuous system is:\\n\\n```wl\\ncoupledContinuousSystem = {\
\\n  D[rho[x, tau], tau] ==\\n    Subscript[d, rho] Laplacian[rho[x, tau], \
{x}] +\\n      Subscript[f, rho][rho[x, tau], nu[x, tau], anisotropy[x, tau], \
resolution[x, tau], x, tau],\\n\\n  D[nu[x, tau], tau] ==\\n    Subscript[d, \
nu] Laplacian[nu[x, tau], {x}] +\\n      Subscript[f, nu][rho[x, tau], nu[x, \
tau], anisotropy[x, tau], resolution[x, tau],\\n        \
measurementOperator[psi[#, tau] &, x], neighborConfiguration, x, tau],\\n\\n  \
D[anisotropy[x, tau], tau] ==\\n    Subscript[f, a][rho[x, tau], nu[x, tau], \
anisotropy[x, tau], resolution[x, tau], neighborConfiguration, x, tau],\\n\\n \
 D[resolution[x, tau], tau] ==\\n    Subscript[f, r][rho[x, tau], nu[x, tau], \
anisotropy[x, tau], resolution[x, tau], x, tau],\\n\\n  D[psi[x, tau], tau] \
==\\n    Subscript[f, psi][rho[x, tau], nu[x, tau], anisotropy[x, tau], \
resolution[x, tau], x, tau] -\\n      measurementOperator[psi[#, tau] &, x] +\
\\n      restorationOperator[measurementOperator[psi[#, tau] &, x], \
x]\\n};\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Effective potential layer\\n\\nA minimal \
potential sourced by signed informational density can be represented \
as:\\n\\n$$\\\\nabla^2\\\\Phi(x)=C\\\\,(\\\\rho(x)-\\\\nu(x))$$\\n\\n```wl\\\
neffectivePotentialEquation =\\n  Laplacian[effectivePotential[x], {x}] == \
Subscript[c, phi] totalDensity[x];\\n```\\n\\nThis lets gravitational-like \
attraction and opposite-side void structure enter through a shared source \
term.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Minimal action extension\\n\\nA first OPF \
extension of the previous action may be written symbolically \
as:\\n\\n$$S=\\\\int \\\\mathcal \
L(\\\\rho,\\\\nu,A,R,\\\\Psi)\\\\,d\\\\Omega$$\\n\\nwith prototype density\\n\
\\n$$\\\\mathcal L = \\\\rho\\\\log(1+\\\\chi_\\\\rho) - \
\\\\nu\\\\log(1+\\\\chi_\\\\nu) + \\\\alpha\\\\,A^2 + \\\\beta\\\\,R^2 + \
\\\\gamma\\\\,\\\\Psi^2 + \\\\eta\\\\,\\\\rho\\\\nu$$\\n\\nThis is only a \
placeholder scaffold to be refined.\\n\\n```wl\\nlagrangianDensity[x_] :=\\n  \
rho[x] Log[1 + Subscript[chi, rho][x]] -\\n  nu[x] Log[1 + Subscript[chi, \
nu][x]] +\\n  Subscript[alpha, a] anisotropy[x]^2 +\\n  Subscript[beta, r] \
resolution[x]^2 +\\n  Subscript[gamma, psi] psi[x]^2 +\\n  Subscript[eta, \
rhonu] rho[x] nu[x];\\n```\\n\\n```wl\\naction = \
Integrate[lagrangianDensity[x], x];\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Immediate \
rigorous notebook update plan\\n\\nThe notebook should now be developed in \
this order:\\n\\n### Step A. Define assumptions\\n\\nSpecify admissibility \
conditions such as:\\n\\n```wl\\nassumptions = {\\n  rho[x] >= 0,\\n  nu[x] \
>= 0,\\n  resolution[x] > 0,\\n  Element[anisotropy[x], Reals],\\n  \
Element[psi[x], Reals]\\n};\\n```\\n\\n### Step B. Decide representation of \
anisotropy\\n\\nChoose one of:\\n\\n- scalar field\\n- vector field\\n- \
tensor field\\n\\nFor first pass, use scalar or vector.\\n\\n### Step C. \
Decide domain\\n\\nChoose one of:\\n\\n- 1D symbolic line\\n- radial \
domain\\n- sphere\\n- full 3D domain\\n\\nFor first rigorous derivation, \
start with **1D or radial**.\\n\\n### Step D. Pick one exact \
prototype\\n\\nBest starting prototype:\\n\\n- 1 spatial variable\\n- \
continuous process-depth variable ``tau``\\n- explicit kernel-free \
operators\\n- no numerical approximation yet\\n\\n### Step E. Derive Euler \
equations\\n\\nUse the action only after deciding whether $$\\\\Psi$$ is \
independent or derived from $$\\\\rho,\\\\nu,A,R$$.\\n\\n### Step F. Build \
tests\\n\\nFor each object define:\\n\\n- well-definedness test\\n- symmetry \
test\\n- stationarity test\\n- stability test\\n- input/output consistency \
test\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. First concrete reduced model to implement \
next\\n\\nThis is the most practical reduced system to analyze first:\\n\\n$$\
\\\\begin{aligned}\\n\\\\partial_\\\\tau \\\\rho &= \
d_\\\\rho\\\\,\\\\partial_x^2\\\\rho + f_\\\\rho(\\\\rho,\\\\nu,A,R),\\\\\\\\\
\\n\\\\partial_\\\\tau \\\\nu &= d_\\\\nu\\\\,\\\\partial_x^2\\\\nu + \
f_\\\\nu(\\\\rho,\\\\nu,A,R,\\\\mathcal M[\\\\Psi]),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_\\\
\\tau \\\\Psi &= f_\\\\Psi(\\\\rho,\\\\nu,A,R)-\\\\mathcal \
M[\\\\Psi]+\\\\mathcal R[\\\\mathcal M[\\\\Psi]],\\\\\\\\\\nD(x) &= \
\\\\rho(x)-\\\\nu(x),\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\partial_x^2 \\\\Phi &= c_\\\\phi D(x).\\n\
\\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nThis is the right first rigorous notebook \
milestone.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. What to do next in this notebook\\n\\nThe \
next update should be one of the following:\\n\\n1. **Reduce the framework to \
a 1D exact symbolic prototype**\\n2. **Define explicit measurement and \
restoration kernels**\\n3. **Specify anisotropy as scalar, vector, or \
tensor**\\n4. **Build an executable test suite around the five-field \
model**\\n\\nRecommended immediate next action:\\n\\n**Create a reduced 1D \
symbolic model with explicit equations and assumptions.**\\n\\nThat gives a \
tractable base for later variational derivation, stability analysis, and \
possible state-space \
reformulation.\\n/exec\\nRESULT\\nNotebookObject[\\\"a0bc2916-c1d7-0341-8721-\
946745d4b453\\\", \
\\\"e2b4fa57-8a0c-7a40-a9a7-07e96be507ce\\\"]\\nENDRESULT(54k08ekrk)\"\>",
